# Public address of the app. REQUIRED on a real deployment: every link in every email is built from it, for
# example APP_URL=https://sign.example.com
# Without it, links are only right when the app runs on your own machine (localhost). The address of a request
# to any other host is not used, because a request header can be forged: links then point at
# http://localhost:3000 and a warning is logged.
# It also gives link previews their picture's address, and emails without a sender's own logo load the GrowSigning
# logo from APP_URL/brand/email-logo.png (until the app is public, mail programs show the words "GrowSigning").
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# How many proxies of your own sit between the internet and the app (a load balancer, a CDN, the router of a
# hosting platform). The caller's address, used for rate limits and the audit trail, is read that many places
# from the right of X-Forwarded-For, because everything further left is whatever the caller typed. Default 1.
# Set 0 only when nothing is in front of the app: the header is then ignored, the app cannot tell callers apart,
# and every visitor shares one set of rate limits. The limit on one-time codes per caller address is then not
# counted at all (the limits per recipient and per mailbox or number still are).
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Where the SQLite database and uploaded files are stored. Use a persistent disk in production.
# DATA_DIR=./data

# Secret for signing cookies and stop links. If unset, one is generated and kept in DATA_DIR.
# APP_SECRET=

# Encrypts every file the app stores from now on (uploaded documents, signed PDFs, files signers attach) with
# AES-256-GCM. 32 random bytes as base64. Make one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# (or: node scripts/encrypt-files.mjs --new-key). Keep a copy apart from the server and its backups: without the key
# nobody can read those files, not even the people who wrote this software. The text read from uploaded PDFs for search is encrypted with
# it too, and so are these values in the database: signatures and initials, what people typed in fields, comments,
# the stored copies of emails, decline reasons, help messages and chats, and public form drafts (src/server/colcrypt.ts).
# The database is NOT encrypted otherwise: names, addresses, subjects and the audit trails stay readable to whoever
# has the disk, so encrypt the disk as well. "npm run setup" writes a new key here into .env (never over one that is set). Files
# stored before stay readable; "node scripts/encrypt-files.mjs" encrypts them (stop the server first, use the same
# settings). Database values stored before are encrypted by the server itself, a batch at a time, or all at once
# with "node scripts/encrypt-columns.mjs" (--check only counts).
# A picture of an ID document at the service counter (Settings, Team, Service counter) can only be kept with this set.
# FILE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Changing the key: put the new one in FILE_ENCRYPTION_KEY and the earlier one here (a list, separated by commas),
# restart, then run the script, which moves every file to the new key. Keep an old key listed until the script says
# it is done. A server that finds files it has no key for stores nothing new until that key is listed here.
# FILE_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_OLD=

# Turning Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OpenDocument, RTF, text, HTML and TIFF uploads into the PDF people sign. Without
# a converter those files are refused with a sentence that says a converter is needed, and nothing offers them.
# soffice: LibreOffice on this machine (soffice on the PATH). gotenberg: a Gotenberg service at CONVERTER_URL.
# The converter must have NO network and should not see this machine's files, because a document can name pictures
# on other servers and on the disk: LibreOffice runs in a box of its own ("unshare -rnmpf") where that works, and the
# log says how far it got when it does not. Read the README, "Turning office
# files into PDF", before switching this on.
# CONVERTER=soffice
# The Gotenberg service inside your own network (files go to it in the clear), for example http://gotenberg:3000
# CONVERTER_URL=
# How long one conversion may take before it is stopped and its processes killed (milliseconds). Default 60000.
# CONVERT_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# How many conversions run at once; the rest wait their turn. Plan for CONVERT_MEMORY_MB of memory each. Default 2.
# CONVERT_MAX_JOBS=2

# Text for pages that are pictures (scans, photos): read by a machine in the background, so they can be searched and
# a screen reader can read them from the signing page's text view, always with a note that a machine read them and
# may have made mistakes. The page that is signed is never changed. Without either setting nothing is read.
# A command on this machine that reads one picture and prints its text. {file} is the picture, {lang} the language
# code (eng, deu, fra...). No shell is used; put a program path with spaces in double quotes. Failures are written to
# the server log; a missing language pack falls back to English. Debian and Ubuntu: apt install tesseract-ocr tesseract-ocr-deu ...
# OCR_CMD=tesseract {file} stdout -l {lang}
# Or a service that takes the PDF (POST, application/pdf, ?lang=eng) and answers with the PDF with a text layer, such
# as OCRmyPDF behind a small web wrapper. The document is sent to it. Used only when OCR_CMD is empty.
# OCRMYPDF_URL=
# How long one page may take before the reader is stopped (milliseconds). Default 60000.
# OCR_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# The most memory one LibreOffice job may ask for, in MB. A tiny file can describe an enormous sheet; with the limit
# such a job fails in seconds instead of taking the machine's memory. 0 switches the limit off. Default 1024.
# CONVERT_MEMORY_MB=1024

# Mail. Without SMTP_URL, emails are only shown in /dev/outbox (development only), nothing is delivered, and the
# audit trail of an envelope says "Not emailed" instead of "Sent to recipient". An email the mail server will not
# take is kept and tried again by the timer below: after 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours, 6 hours and a day.
# A mail server that stops answering is given up on after 10 seconds to connect, 10 for its greeting and 20 of
# silence, and the email is tried again later; add ?connectionTimeout=..&greetingTimeout=..&socketTimeout=..
# (milliseconds) to the address to change that. Emails about money are written down inside the account's billing
# step and handed to the mail server after it (a few at a time), so a slow mail server never holds up Cancel, a
# refund or a payment message; one that was never handed over (the server stopped) is sent by the timer.
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com
# MAIL_FROM="GrowSigning" <sign@example.com>
# Hand no email to the mail server in SMTP_URL without encryption. With 1, an smtp:// address must offer STARTTLS,
# or the email is not handed over: it is kept and tried again, and /security says the rule is on. (An smtps://
# address is encrypted from the first byte either way.) This covers one step, from this server to your mail
# provider. How the provider carries the email on to each recipient is decided there and on the recipient's side
# (MTA-STS or REQUIRETLS at the provider), not here, and it is never end to end encryption.
# SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=1
# What became of an email after the mail server took it: delivered, bounced, marked as spam. Set a secret of at
# least 16 characters (openssl rand -hex 24) and give your mail provider (Postmark, SendGrid, Mailgun, Resend, or
# Amazon SES through SNS) the webhook address https://<your domain>/api/mail/events?token=<the secret>. Senders then
# hear at once when an email to a recipient could not be delivered. Without it that address does not exist. A secret
# with "+" in it (openssl rand -base64) also works pasted into the address as it is. See the README, "Knowing when
# an email did not arrive".
# MAIL_EVENTS_TOKEN=

# Lets an external scheduler run reminders, new tries for emails that did not leave, expiry, nudges and account closing:
# POST /api/cron with "Authorization: Bearer <secret>". It also expires signatures whose date has passed (a month's at
# its end, top-ups 12 months after their payment, the trial's after 30 days) and emails top-ups 30 and 3 days before
# they expire. Call it at least once an hour, so those happen on time.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Paying (optional). Without these nothing is counted and there is nothing to buy.
# STRIPE_PRICE_GROVE is the monthly price. It must charge the amount in src/lib/impact.ts.
# Send these events to /api/stripe/webhook: checkout.session.completed, checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded,
# checkout.session.async_payment_failed, invoice.paid, invoice.upcoming, invoice.payment_failed,
# customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted, charge.refunded, charge.dispute.created.
# 1 asks business buyers for their tax number (VAT, GST) and billing address on the payment page, for the invoice.
# Try it in test mode first: the provider refuses the request unless its own settings allow it.
# STRIPE_COLLECT_TAX_ID=1
# How long a message from the provider is worked on before it is answered (milliseconds). Longer work is
# finished in the background from an inbox table. Default 5000.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_WAIT_MS=5000
# Pin the provider's API version to the one you tested with (and set the webhook endpoint to the same one).
# Unset, a production server logs a loud warning once, because the account's default version can change.
# STRIPE_API_VERSION=
# Card disputes up to this many dollars are accepted with the provider automatically; larger ones wait for a person.
# 0 leaves every dispute to a person. Billing stops either way.
# ACCEPT_DISPUTES_UP_TO_USD=25
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_GROVE=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Taking payments while signing (optional), into each sending account's OWN Stripe account (Stripe Connect).
# GrowSigning is never the merchant, never sees card details and takes no fee. Without all three, nothing offers it.
# Not offered with BILLING_MODE=deployment. In the Stripe dashboard of the platform account: Connect, Settings, the
# client id (ca_...) and the redirect address https://<APP_URL host>/api/collect/callback. Then a Connect webhook
# endpoint ("events on connected accounts") at /api/collect/webhook with: checkout.session.completed,
# checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded, checkout.session.async_payment_failed, checkout.session.expired,
# charge.refunded, charge.dispute.created, charge.dispute.updated, charge.dispute.closed, account.application.deauthorized.
# STRIPE_CONNECT_SECRET_KEY is the platform's secret key; unset, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is used.
# STRIPE_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
# STRIPE_CONNECT_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Codes that make the monthly plan free for whoever enters one, at sign-up or on the Plan page. Comma separated.
# Default: GROWalition. Set to an empty value to have no codes.
# FREE_PLAN_CODES=GROWalition

# ---- Help, trust and the people who run the service ----------------------------------------------

# The address people can write to. Shown on /help, told about every new help request and every late one, and
# sent the alerts that need a human: an unmatched payment, a card dispute. A help request is answered in the
# operator queue (/app/operator), not by replying to that email. Without this address, new and late requests
# are emailed to OPERATOR_EMAILS instead, and are shown in the operator queue either way.
# When set, it is also printed in the footer of every public page and in the menu of the app. A production
# server that starts without it logs a loud warning, because every page promises that a person answers.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=help@example.com

# Where reports of suspicious documents go, and disputes ("I did not sign this, or this was not me"), which
# also go to OPERATOR_EMAILS and into the operator queue. Default: SUPPORT_EMAIL.
# ABUSE_EMAIL=abuse@example.com

# Who may open /app/operator (help requests, reports, pausing an account, closing a dispute: until a person
# here closes it, a disputed envelope cannot be deleted for good). Comma separated. Each address must belong to
# an account whose email is confirmed. Default: nobody.
# OPERATOR_EMAILS=you@example.com,colleague@example.com

# The published response time for help requests, in real hours (nights and weekends count). The help page,
# the confirmation email and every request quote the actual time a person will have answered by. When it
# passes, the request says "We are late" to the customer and the timer emails SUPPORT_EMAIL and every operator.
# Default 24. Urgent requests (cannot log in, charged wrongly, a stuck document or a near deadline) have their
# own, default 8, never longer than the first. With neither SUPPORT_EMAIL nor OPERATOR_EMAILS set, no time is
# promised anywhere.
# SUPPORT_FIRST_REPLY_HOURS=24
# SUPPORT_URGENT_REPLY_HOURS=8

# The help chat ("Ask for help" on every page, "Help" in the app and on the signing page). With none of the keys
# below it answers from built-in answers and nothing typed into it leaves the server. With one, an AI model
# answers, by default Kimi K3 (Moonshot AI's open-weights model), and what people type into the chat goes to that
# host: /security then lists it under "Where your data is". First match wins:
#   SUPPORT_CHAT_API_KEY  any host that speaks the OpenAI chat API, at SUPPORT_CHAT_BASE_URL (https only; plain
#                         http only to this machine itself, for a model run locally, and under DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE=
#                         government or OUTSIDE_CALLS=listed/none also to a private network address). Default
#                         OpenRouter, which those two settings never use (see DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE below).
#   MOONSHOT_API_KEY      Moonshot AI's own API (https://api.moonshot.ai/v1, model kimi-k3)
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY    OpenRouter (model moonshotai/kimi-k3-20260715)
# SUPPORT_CHAT_MODEL overrides the model for any of them.
# SUPPORT_CHAT_API_KEY=
# SUPPORT_CHAT_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# SUPPORT_CHAT_MODEL=moonshotai/kimi-k3-20260715
# MOONSHOT_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Questions the model answers for everyone in a day (UTC). Past it the built-in answers reply. 0 sends none, and
# the chat then says the built-in help answers, as without a key.
# Default 2000. One internet address gets 8 a minute and 60 a day, a signing link 40 a day, an account 300 a day.
# SUPPORT_CHAT_DAILY_LIMIT=2000
# Writing a document in the editor ("No document yet? Write one") can ask the same model for a draft or a change,
# on accounts whose AI switch is on. Drafts and changes together, per account per day (UTC); they also count
# against the limits above. 0 leaves only writing by hand and the starters. Default 30.
# WRITE_AI_DAILY_LIMIT=30
# AI help when sending and signing, with the same model: "Draft a message" in step 3 and, on accounts whose
# admin switched on "Let the AI read document text" (off by default), suggested fields in step 2 and a plain
# summary for signers. Suggestions, drafts and summaries together, per account per day (UTC); they also count
# against the help chat's limits. 0 switches this help off. Default 100.
# AI_ASSIST_DAILY_LIMIT=100
# Key dates (an account's own switch, after "Let the AI read document text"): how many completed documents the timer
# reads for their dates and amounts in one run, across the server. Each counts as one AI request of its account's
# day, and the timer uses at most half of an account's day (AI_ASSIST_DAILY_LIMIT), so people keep the other half;
# with a limit of 1 it reads nothing by itself. 0 stops it reading by itself; senders can still press "Find the dates
# and amounts". Default 3.
# KEY_DATES_PER_TICK=3
# Who runs the model and in which country it handles what people type, as /security prints it. The name is
# known for OpenRouter and Moonshot AI; the country is not, and the page says so until it is set.
# SUPPORT_CHAT_PROVIDER_NAME=
# SUPPORT_CHAT_PROVIDER_COUNTRY=

# Days after a payment during which its unused signatures (not expired) are refunded on request, with no approval. Default 30.
# The help page and the pricing section quote this number.
# REFUND_DAYS=30

# How many people an account that has not paid may send to per day during its first week. Default 50.
# "Has not paid" means: no payment for the plan or a top-up that is at least 14 days old and was neither
# refunded nor disputed (PAID_TRUST_DAYS in src/lib/policy.ts), and not marked as trusted in the operator
# queue. A small amount the software sent back by itself because more was charged than we said does not count as
# a refund here. An account that paid yesterday still has the limit. Everyone a sender newly emails counts: a first
# send, a person added or an address changed on an envelope that is out, a bulk row. The visitors of a public
# form are counted apart, against the same number for each form. Team invitations have their own limit of
# 20 people a day for each account.
# NEW_ACCOUNT_DAILY_RECIPIENTS=50

# The same limit after the first week. Default 200. A payment lifts both limits once it has stood for 14
# days, and "Trust this account" in the operator queue lifts them at once. The same holds for the filter
# for fake payment notices. The help and security pages quote both numbers.
# UNPAID_DAILY_RECIPIENTS=200

# How many different recipients must report an account within 30 days before its sending is paused until a
# person has reviewed it. Default 3. Open reports and reports an operator confirmed as abuse both count.
# Reports from visitors of a public form are shown to operators and never count.
# ABUSE_SUSPEND_AFTER=3

# Hours after which an unfinished start from a public form that nobody touched is closed, so it no longer
# counts against what the form can still take. The visitor can simply start again. Default 24. Never a start
# whose visitor pressed Finish while no signature was free: they were told that what they filled in is kept,
# so it stays open until its deadline and they are emailed once the form can take their signature.
# POWERFORM_IDLE_HOURS=24
# The same for a public form marked as filled in on a shared device (a front-desk tablet), in minutes.
# POWERFORM_SHARED_IDLE_MINUTES=60

# How many click-to-agree acceptances one account can record in a calendar month (UTC), over all its terms, counted
# apart for visitors who type their own details and for those the website vouches for (a signed link or the API), so
# strangers typing on a page cannot use up what the website needs. An acceptance is not a signature and costs
# nothing, so this is what keeps a page anyone can open from filling the records. The admins are emailed at 80
# percent of either count and when it is reached; after that those visitors are told the terms cannot be accepted
# right now and the API answers 429, until the 1st. Default 1000. 0 records none.
# CLICKWRAP_MONTHLY_LIMIT=1000

# A paid plan that has sent nothing for this many days is not renewed: the renewal is stopped and the owner is
# told. Default 90. Set to 0 to only send a warning.
# DORMANT_AUTO_STOP_DAYS=90

# How many days a line of an account's security record (Settings, Security) is kept: a whole number from 1 to 3650.
# The same for every account, so an admin cannot shorten it to wipe what they did. Older lines are deleted by the
# timer (POST /api/cron); until then database triggers refuse to change, replace or delete a line, unless its account
# is deleted. The fingerprints of the lines are keyed with APP_SECRET: keep it out of the database's backups, so that
# someone who can write the database alone cannot rewrite the record unnoticed.
# After APP_SECRET was changed, give the one before here (several separated by commas) so that the lines of the
# security record written under it still check. Used for nothing else. See docs/runbooks.md, "Rotating APP_SECRET".
# APP_SECRET_RETIRED=
# Default 400.
# SECURITY_LOG_DAYS=400
# A folder outside the database for a daily note of where every account's security record stood: one file a day,
# one line per account (its id, the number and fingerprint of its newest line, the time; no names, no words).
# With it, Settings, Security also holds the record against the newest 7 of those files, so an older database put
# back, or a record rewritten by someone with the database and APP_SECRET, shows as changed. Worth as much as the
# folder: copy it away or make it write-once, apart from the database and its backups. Unset: nothing is written.
# SECURITY_HEADS_DIR=/var/lib/growsigning-heads
# Every new password (sign-up, the emailed link, Settings) is looked up in the Pwned Passwords list of passwords from
# data leaks, and refused when it is there. Only the first 5 characters of the password's SHA-1 fingerprint are sent;
# the list answers with every ending it knows for them, and the comparison happens on this server. No address, name
# or whole fingerprint leaves. With no answer in 3 seconds, or a network that refuses the call, the password is allowed
# and nothing says it was checked. On by default; 0 switches it off. OUTSIDE_CALLS=none switches it off as well,
# unless the host of the list is in OUTBOUND_ALLOW.
# PWNED_CHECK=1
# Another copy of the list, for example a mirror inside a closed network: an http(s) address that answers
# GET <address><5 characters> as the Pwned Passwords range API does. Default https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/.
# HIBP_RANGE_URL=

# Hours a signing request may sit unopened before the sender is told. Default 48.
# UNOPENED_ALERT_HOURS=48

# ---- Signing in with Google or Microsoft (optional) ----------------------------------------------

# "Continue with Google" on the sign-in and sign-up pages and under Settings. Both values must be set. With either
# one missing, nothing about Google is shown anywhere and its routes answer 404.
# Create an OAuth client of the type "Web application" in the Google Cloud console and register this redirect
# address there, exactly: APP_URL + /api/auth/google/callback
# for example https://sign.example.com/api/auth/google/callback
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# "Continue with Microsoft". Both values must be set. Register an app in the Microsoft Entra admin centre, add the
# platform "Web" and this redirect address, exactly: APP_URL + /api/auth/microsoft/callback
# for example https://sign.example.com/api/auth/microsoft/callback
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Who may sign in with Microsoft: common (work, school and personal accounts, the default), organizations (work and
# school), consumers (personal), or the id of one organization (its "Directory (tenant) ID"). A domain name is not
# accepted: Microsoft sign-in then stays off and the server log says why.
# MICROSOFT_TENANT=common

# A person is recognised by the provider's own id for them, never by an email address alone. The README says when
# a provider is taken to vouch for an address ("Signing in with Google or Microsoft").

# Single sign-on for an organisation (Settings, Security, "Single sign-on"): each account adds its own OpenID
# Connect service and claims its email domains with a DNS record; nothing is set here for it. It exists only when
# FILE_ENCRYPTION_KEY is set, because the client secret of each service is kept encrypted with it. Each organisation
# registers this redirect address in its own service: APP_URL + /api/sso/callback
# SAML 2.0 is the other kind of service in the same card (no setting here either): each account gives its
# identity provider APP_URL + /api/sso/saml/sp/<id shown on the card> as entity id and APP_URL + /api/sso/saml/acs
# as reply address. Members from a directory (SCIM 2.0) use APP_URL + /api/scim/v2 with a token made on the card.
# How long a browser of someone at a domain where "everyone signs in through" the service stays signed in, in
# hours (1 to 720), so that leaving the organisation ends their access here. Default 12.
# SSO_SESSION_HOURS=12

# ---- Where the data is (printed on /security and in the data processing summary) ------------------

# Plain text, printed as written. The page lists a mail provider, a payment provider and a text message provider
# only when they are switched on above, so it cannot name one this server does not use. Whatever you leave empty
# here is printed as "The operator of this server has not said ... Ask them.", never left out, and a production
# server that starts with something missing says so once in its log.
# Who runs this server: legal name and country. A customer who needs a signed data processing agreement signs it
# with them.
# OPERATOR_NAME=Example Trees e.V., Hamburg, Germany
# Where the server and its files are, and the hosting company.
# DATA_REGION=Frankfurt, Germany
# DATA_HOST=Example Hosting GmbH
# Whether and where backups are kept, and for how long. One sentence. Also quoted where Settings and the document
# page say what stays after a deletion (src/lib/retention.ts): deleting the files of a document does not reach backups.
# DATA_BACKUPS=A copy every night, kept for 30 days in Helsinki, Finland.
# The company behind SMTP_URL and the country it handles email in.
# MAIL_PROVIDER_NAME=Example Mail Ltd
# MAIL_PROVIDER_COUNTRY=Ireland
# The country of the Stripe company your payment account is with, as your agreement with Stripe names it.
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER_COUNTRY=
# The text message provider, when texts are switched on. Its name is known when it is Twilio.
# SMS_PROVIDER_NAME=
# SMS_PROVIDER_COUNTRY=

# ---- Text messages (optional) --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Text messages are off until one of the two ways below is set up. While they are off nothing in the product
# offers them: no one-time code by text message, no "also text the signing link", and the API refuses both with a
# sentence that says so. A one-time code by email needs none of this.
#
# (a) Twilio. All of TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID and TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN, and one of TWILIO_FROM (a number of yours in
#     international form) or TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID (wins when both are set). Texts are sent with fetch to
#     https://api.twilio.com, no SDK.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+15550100
# TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=
#
# (b) Any other provider, through a small hook of your own. Every text is one POST to SMS_WEBHOOK_URL with the
#     JSON body {"to": "+447700900123", "text": "..."} and the header "Authorization: Bearer <SMS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN>".
#     Answer 2xx when you took the text (an "id" in a JSON answer is kept as its reference). Answer 400 or 422 for
#     a number that can never be texted: that is final and the sender is told at once. Any other failure is tried
#     again by the timer, after 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours, 6 hours and a day.
# SMS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://texts.example.com/send
# SMS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
#
# The most text message parts one account can cause in 24 hours: codes, links and reminders, every new one counted,
# also a code that is asked for again. It counts parts, as providers bill them: most of our texts are 1 or 2 parts,
# and a long text, or one with a character outside the GSM alphabet (Arabic, Amharic: 70 characters a part), counts
# several. A text goes only when all of its parts fit in what is left, and a send to several people adds up every
# text it would send, so the number is never passed. Curly quotes and long dashes in a subject are sent as plain
# ones, so they do not double the parts. A text costs you money and customers are not charged for it, so this is a
# hard stop with a clear message. Default 200. 0 lets none through.
# SMS_DAILY_PER_ACCOUNT=200

# ---- WhatsApp and codes by phone call (optional) -------------------------------------------------

# The signing link also by WhatsApp, for someone the sender says agreed to it (src/server/whatsapp.ts). Off until
# one of these is set; then the editor offers "Also send the link by WhatsApp" under "How they get it".
# (a) The Meta WhatsApp Business Platform: a verified business, its own number, and approved message templates.
#     Register the four texts printed in README "WhatsApp and codes by phone call" as templates with three blanks
#     ({{1}} sender, {{2}} document, {{3}} link) and put their names here. Without WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_INVITE it is off.
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_INVITE=
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_REMIND=
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_DONE=
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_DEADLINE=        (optional: the reminder template is used for "closes soon" without it)
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=en
#     Replies (STOP) and delivery reports come to APP_URL/api/whatsapp/webhook. Meta signs them with the app secret:
#     without WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET they are refused, so nobody could stop the messages by replying. Set it.
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=              (any text: Meta sends it once when you set up the webhook)
# (b) A Twilio WhatsApp sender, with the Twilio settings above: TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM is its number. Set the
#     status callback and the incoming message address of the sender to APP_URL/api/whatsapp/webhook. A template
#     setting that starts with HX is used as a Twilio content template; otherwise the text itself is sent.
# TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM=+14155238886
# The most WhatsApp messages one account can cause in 24 hours. Default 100. 0 lets none through. Messages cost
# you money (per message, set by Meta or Twilio) and customers are not charged for them, so this caps the cost.
# WHATSAPP_DAILY_PER_ACCOUNT=100
# On a development machine, WHATSAPP_DEV_OUTBOX=1 writes messages to the outbox instead (never in production).
# WHATSAPP_DEV_OUTBOX=1
#
# "Call me with the code" on the code screen: we call the number and read a one-time code out slowly, twice, for a
# landline or someone who cannot read a text (src/server/voice.ts). Needs Twilio Programmable Voice with
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID, TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN and TWILIO_FROM (a number that can make calls). A call counts as one text
# against SMS_DAILY_PER_ACCOUNT. On a development machine it works with SMS_DEV_OUTBOX=1 and writes to the outbox.
# VOICE_ENABLED=1

# ---- The digital seal of a completed PDF (optional) ------------------------------------------------

# With a seal key set, every PDF completed from then on carries an invisible PAdES signature of whoever runs this
# server, so anyone can open it in Acrobat or another reader and see that it has not changed since. It is a seal of
# the server, not a signature of the people who signed. Files completed earlier are never sealed afterwards. Without
# these settings nothing changes: the signed PDF is byte for byte what it was.
# Acrobat calls the seal valid only when the certificate comes from an authority on the Adobe Approved Trust List or
# an EU trusted list (a purchase). With any other certificate it says the validity is unknown.
#
# Either a .p12 or .pfx file with the key and its certificates (RSA of 2048 bits or more, or ECDSA P-256):
# SEAL_P12=/etc/growsigning/seal.p12
# SEAL_P12_PASSWORD=
#
# or the same as PEM: the private key (not encrypted), the seal certificate, and the certificates that issued it.
# Each is a path to a file, or the PEM text itself (with \n for the line breaks).
# SEAL_KEY_PEM=/etc/growsigning/seal-key.pem
# SEAL_CERT_PEM=/etc/growsigning/seal-cert.pem
# SEAL_CHAIN_PEM=/etc/growsigning/seal-chain.pem
#
# Sealing is refused, and PDFs are made as before, when the certificate has expired or is not valid yet, its key
# usage allows neither digitalSignature nor nonRepudiation, the key does not belong to it, or the certificate that
# issued it is not given. The reason is logged and filed in the operator queue (and emailed to SUPPORT_EMAIL and
# OPERATOR_EMAILS) once.
#
# A time-stamp service (RFC 3161), so the seal carries a time from it (PAdES B-T) and not only this server's clock
# (B-B). Several, separated by commas, are tried in order, 3 seconds each and 4 in all, because the last signer's
# Finish waits for it (TSA_URLS is read the same way). A service that did not answer in time is not asked again for
# 10 minutes by this server process, so signers who finish later do not wait for it; files completed meanwhile are
# sealed without a time-stamp. A restart, or a second process, waits once more. A time more than 5 minutes from this server's clock is not
# used. When none gives a time-stamp that checks out, the PDF is sealed at B-B at once and its certificate of
# completion says so. The user name and password go to every address listed, and only over https: with them set, a
# plain http address is not asked.
# TSA_URL=https://timestamp.example.com/tsa
# TSA_USER=
# TSA_PASSWORD=
# A PEM file of the certificates the service's own certificate must lead to. Without it, the time-stamp's own
# signature, its certificate's purpose and its time are checked, but not who issued that certificate: then nothing
# calls the time stamp "trusted", and the Verify page does not show its time. Set it to say "trusted time stamp".
# TSA_TRUST=/etc/growsigning/tsa-roots.pem
#
# With TSA_URL set, two more things happen when the last person finishes (src/server/auditstamp.ts, ltv.ts):
# - The record's chain head is time-stamped first, before the certificate is drawn, and the certificate says so in
#   one line. It shares the 4 seconds above with the seal's own time stamp.
# - After a seal with a time stamp, each certificate of the seal and of the time-stamp service is checked at the
#   addresses it names (OCSP, else its revocation list), and the answers go into the file (PAdES B-LT), then one time
#   stamp over the whole file (B-LTA). Certificates that name no address get nothing added. LTV_WAIT_MS is how long
#   all those answers together may take, because the last signer's Finish waits for the stored file (most answer in
#   well under a second). When one does not answer in time, the file stays at B-T and the page of the document says so.
#   The time stamp over the whole file shares the same time. 0 turns this step off. With OUTSIDE_CALLS=listed or none,
#   add the hosts the certificates name (their OCSP services and revocation lists) to OUTBOUND_ALLOW.
# LTV_WAIT_MS=20000

# ---- Archive format: completed files as PDF/A (optional) ------------------------------------------
# With VERAPDF_CMD set, account admins see "Archive format (PDF/A)" under Settings, Records and data, More options
# (off by default).
# With it on, documents completed from then on are made as PDF/A-2b, then sealed, and veraPDF checks the stored file
# (src/server/pdfa.ts). A file is called PDF/A only when veraPDF passed it; otherwise the document is made as a normal
# PDF and its certificate says why. Without VERAPDF_CMD nothing changes and the switch is not shown.
# VERAPDF_CMD is the veraPDF command line program (it needs Java), with any arguments it needs, split at spaces. The
# server adds "--flavour 2b --format mrr <file>", and asks "--version" once. It runs with only PATH, JAVA_HOME and a
# language set, so none of this server's secrets reach it. At most 2 checks run at once (each is a Java program of a
# few hundred MB). Install it from https://verapdf.org/software/.
# VERAPDF_CMD=/opt/verapdf/verapdf
# How long one check may take before it is stopped and the file is made as a normal PDF. The signer is not kept
# waiting: the signed file and the "completed" emails follow once the check is done. Between 5000 and 600000;
# default 60000.
# VERAPDF_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# ---- Running it yourself: outside connections and the security contact (all optional) -------------
# Which other machines the server may connect to (src/lib/outside.ts; /security prints the list).
#   all     the default: every service you switched on, and the webhook addresses account admins enter.
#   listed  only the hosts your settings imply, plus OUTBOUND_ALLOW; webhooks only to WEBHOOK_ALLOW_HOSTS.
#   none    for a closed network: only the mail relay in SMTP_URL, plus OUTBOUND_ALLOW. Payments, Google and
#           Microsoft sign-in, text messages, time-stamps and an AI model on another host are switched off at
#           start, and each page shows them as absent.
# Any other value is read as none. This is a second line of defence inside the software, not a firewall: deny
# egress in the network around the server as well.
# OUTSIDE_CALLS=all
# More hosts the server may connect to in listed and none, separated by commas ("*.example.org" is any name under it).
# OUTBOUND_ALLOW=
# In listed and none, the only hosts a webhook may go to. Empty there means no webhooks at all.
# WEBHOOK_ALLOW_HOSTS=

# A public body running its own copy: "government" (src/lib/deployment.ts). Then the signing record never carries
# trees or the paper estimate, and the help chat never goes to OpenRouter or Moonshot AI: a model answers only once
# SUPPORT_CHAT_API_KEY and SUPPORT_CHAT_BASE_URL point at one you run yourself, at a private network address (plain
# http allowed there) or at a host listed in OUTBOUND_ALLOW. OUTSIDE_CALLS=listed or none applies the same rule to
# the help chat (src/lib/chatboundary.ts; how to run one: docs/self-hosted-model.md). The screens stay as they are: admins
# switch "Trees on screens and emails" and "AI answers in the help chat" under Settings, Account. It certifies
# nothing. Unset is the ordinary server; any other value runs as unset, and the log says so at start.
# DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE=

# How this server is paid for (src/lib/billingmode.ts). "signatures" (the default) is the consumer product: signatures
# are counted, held and expire, and paying goes through Stripe. "deployment" is for a public body paying one fixed
# fee a year under a contract: nothing is counted, held or expires, sending has no signature limit and no daily
# limit on people (the pause and the fake-notice filter stay; a person clears an account), the STRIPE_*
# settings are ignored and the payment provider is never called, the plan's routes answer 404, and Settings, Plan
# shows one line: "This service is provided under <LICENSE_HOLDER>'s agreement <AGREEMENT_REF>, valid until
# <AGREEMENT_UNTIL>." Each of the three is optional and left out of the sentence when unset. Nothing stops on the
# date: a date that has passed is said as passed. No invoice is sent; invoice through your own systems. Public pages
# then show no consumer prices, top-ups or trees per signature.
# BILLING_MODE=signatures
# LICENSE_HOLDER=
# AGREEMENT_REF=
# AGREEMENT_UNTIL=2027-12-31

# Where to report a security weakness: an email address or an https address, several separated by commas. Served
# at /.well-known/security.txt (RFC 9116) and shown on /security/disclosure. Empty: there is no security.txt, and
# the page sends people to the help address instead.
# SECURITY_CONTACT=security@example.org
# The number of days (1 to 90) within which you answer such a report. The page promises exactly this, in your
# name, and only when it is set. Empty: the page says that no time was stated.
# SECURITY_REPLY_DAYS=

# ---- The home page -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The video on the home page: Treegens' AI counting freshly planted mangroves in a planter's video. Left unset, the
# video that ships with the product is shown (public/videos/tree-count.mp4). Set an mp4 or webm of your own, as an
# https address or as a file under public/videos/ written /videos/name.mp4, or "none" for no video block. Anything
# else (a page on a video site, another kind of file, a file name under public/videos/ that is not there, an http://
# address when APP_URL is https) gives no block rather than an empty player. A file at another address is not
# fetched to check it. The caption says
# what the video shows, so set only a video that shows that. It never plays by itself and starts muted.
# HOME_VIDEO_URL=
# The still picture shown before a video of your own plays (webp, jpg, png or avif; same two kinds of address).
# With one, nothing of the video is fetched until someone presses play.
# HOME_VIDEO_POSTER=

# ---- Development only ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Switch text messages on without a provider: every text is only written to the outbox (/dev/outbox), where you
# can read the code or follow the link. Ignored on a production server unless DEV_OUTBOX=1 is set as well, because
# it delivers nothing to anyone.
# SMS_DEV_OUTBOX=1

# Accept webhook addresses that are not public https, for testing against localhost.
# ALLOW_PRIVATE_WEBHOOKS=1

# Show the outbox in a production build. Never set this on a public server: it exposes every signing link.
# DEV_OUTBOX=1
